39 | woodlots | Park historian's 1939 recommendation for the west slope: thin the brush, leave scattered medium-height pine and oak | - | ~1939 (recommendation of the park historian, carried out by the late 1940s; not an 1863 observation) | stated | The report says that, relying on the Warren map and Brady and Tipton photographs, Tilberg recommended opening the view from the summit by thinning out the brush of the western slope and leaving a scattered growth of medium-height pine and oak | =Relying on the Warren map and Brady/Tipton photographs, Tilberg recommended opening the view from the summit of Little Round Top by thinning out the brush of the western slope and leaving a scattered growth of medium-height pine and oak. | =GNMP’s staff historian Frederick Tilberg undertook the first historical landscape study of the vistas from Little Round Top, reporting on his findings in 1939
39 | woodlots | Tall trees removed in the 1930s-40s to open vistas toward Devil's Den, the Wheatfield and the Trostle farm; Plum Run swale cleared | - | ~1939-1940s (vista cutting based on a historical study of the vistas) | stated | The report says that a considerable number of taller trees were removed to open the line of sight to Devil's Den, vistas were cleared of tall trees toward the Wheatfield and the Trostle Farm, and the Plum Run swale at the northwestern foot was cleared for a view from Sedgwick Avenue westward to the Wheatfield; these are later clearings made to reopen the views that existed at the time of the battle | =A considerable number of taller trees were removed to open the line of sight from Little Round Top to the Devil’s Den. Vistas were also cleared of tall trees in the direction of the Wheatfield and the Trostle Farm. The Plum Run “swale” at the northwestern foot of Little Round Top was cleared to open a view from Sedgwick Avenue westward to the Wheatfield. | =GNMP’s staff historian Frederick Tilberg undertook the first historical landscape study of the vistas from Little Round Top, reporting on his findings in 1939

40 | fences | Four fence types identified for the park as those used by farmers at the time of the battle | - | 1863-07 (types identified by park research of 1961; adopted in the Mission 66 era) | stated | The report says park research identified four types of fences, based on historic types in use by farmers at the time of the battle in 1863: Virginia worm fence, post-and-rail fence, stone-and-rider fence and stone wall | =Another Mission 66-era initiative was the restoration of fences and breastworks. This work was guided by historical research identifying four types of fences for use within the park based on historic types in use by farmers at the time of the battle in 1863. The four fence types adopted were Virginia worm fence, post-and-rail fence, stone-and-rider fence and stone wall. | =Frederick Tilberg, “Location and Type of Fences on the Battlefield of Gettysburg,” December 27, 1961.
40 | fences | Mission 66 fences restored to the locations of the Warren map; some used to enclose pasture | - | ~Mission 66 era (fences restored to the Warren-map locations; not 1863 fabric) | stated | The report says efforts were made to restore these fences to the locations indicated by the Warren map, although they were also sometimes used for other reasons such as to enclose pasture for the park's agricultural lease program | =Efforts were made to restore these fences to locations indicated by the Warren map, although they were also sometimes used for other reasons such as to enclose pasture for the park’s agricultural lease program. | =Frederick Tilberg, “Location and Type of Fences on the Battlefield of Gettysburg,” December 27, 1961.
42 | fences | 1970s pasture: worm fences along the roads and stone-rider fences on the breastworks, features that had not existed in July 1863 | - | !1977 (pasture, worm fences and stone-rider fences of the 1970s; the report says they had not existed in July 1863) | stated | The report says that in 1977 the western slope was cleared and livestock introduced, worm fences were built along the roadways to enclose the pasture and the breastworks at the east end of the pasture were converted into stone-rider fences, introducing features that had not existed in July 1863 | =control techniques in 1977, the western slope of Little Round Top was cleared and an attempt made to control vegetation through the introduction of livestock. Cows and sheep were pastured in the Plum Run Valley but they proved ineffective at controlling vegetation growth. Worm fences were built along the roadways to enclose the pasture and the breastworks at the east end of the pasture were converted into stone-rider fences, with the result of introducing features into the landscape that were confusing to some visitors and had not existed in July, 1863.

